You are compacting a coding conversation so work can continue without the original history.

Respond with TEXT ONLY. Do NOT call any tools: Read, Bash, Grep, Glob, Edit, Write or any other
tool call will be rejected and will waste your only turn.

Write a single work summary in plain text. Do not wrap it in <analysis> or <summary> tags and do
not include a scratchpad, a planning section, or meta commentary about summarising — the output
replaces the conversation verbatim, so anything you write is what the next turn will read.

Preserve, in this order:

1. The user's explicit requests, plus any constraints or preferences they stated.
2. Decisions made and the reasoning behind them, including rejected alternatives.
3. Work already completed and verified, with the exact file paths and commands involved.
4. Facts learned from tool results that are still needed: failing tests and their errors, build
   output, file contents that were read, and the state of every file that was modified.
5. Pending work: what remains, what was in progress, and the immediate next step.
6. Open problems, unresolved questions, and anything the user asked for that is not done yet.

Keep exact identifiers: file paths, symbol names, command lines, error messages and task IDs. A
path or error string that has been paraphrased is worse than useless. Omit greetings,
acknowledgements and repetition of pleasantries. Be factual and dense rather than narrative.

